    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d764">

                <pb facs="0820" n="806" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg806"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00115 European War (1939)/8860:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-15T12:00:00-05:00">March 15,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 6:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>498. Minister of War<anchor n="9" xml:id="d764-f1"/> informed me this morning
                    that Acting President Echandía<anchor n="10" xml:id="d764-f2"/> has issued
                    instructions to Director General of National Police<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d764-f3"/> to effect internment of enemy nationals with the greatest
                    urgency. General Espinel said that in his opinion the internment will take place
                    this week.</p>
                <p>I emphasized our desire that the more dangerous Germans should be deported to
                    Panama in accordance with agreement already reached between the Colombian and
                    Panamanian Governments.</p>
                <p>General Espinel said that he is writing me a letter requesting under Lend-Lease
                    four Douglas transport planes which are urgently needed. He said that a formal
                    request has been or is about to be made by the Colombian Embassy in Washington.
                    I said that I would be glad to endorse the request as both Colonel
                        Densford<anchor n="12" xml:id="d764-f4"/> and Colonel Weeks<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d764-f5"/> are convinced of the need of these planes but that I felt
                    certain the <pb facs="0821" n="807" xml:id="pg807"/> Colombian Government would
                    have a greater chance to obtain this equipment if favorable action were taken
                    with respect to enemy aliens.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d764-f1" n="9" xml:id="d764-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Domingo Espinel succeeded Gonzalo Restrepo as Colombian Minister of War in
                    March 1944.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d764-f2" n="10" xml:id="d764-f2-content">
                <p>Dario Echandía. As first designate, he exercised presidential powers while
                    President Alfonso López was in the United States from November 19, 1943, to May
                    16, 1944.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d764-f3" n="11" xml:id="d764-f3-content">
                <p>José Joaquín Caicedo Castilla.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d764-f4" n="12" xml:id="d764-f4-content">
                <p>Col. Charles F. Densford, successor of Col. Melvin G. Faris as Chief of the
                    American Military Mission in Colombia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d764-f5" n="13" xml:id="d764-f5-content">
                <p>Col. John A. Weeks, Military Attaché.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
